#original-page(595.276pt, 841.89pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(519.292pt, 793.74pt, "9")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 116.705pt, "Successful mechanical exfoliation requires a core condition: the intrinsic work of adhesion at the ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 136.8pt, "target interface must be significantly lower than the critical fracture toughness of the film or ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 156.966pt, "substrate, ensuring cracks propagate preferentially along the interface without damaging the ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 177.062pt, "substrate material. This method avoids chemical processing, and is particularly suitable for ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 197.216pt, "materials with inherent cleavage planes, such as GaN and mica. The reusability of the substrate ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 217.323pt, "further reduces manufacturing costs, while the process enables the fabrication of high-performance ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 237.477pt, "functional films. However, its applicability is constrained by several factors, including high ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 257.585pt, "sensitivity to interfacial properties, strict requirements for uniform crack initiation and propagation, ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 277.739pt, "and the inevitable presence of residual substrate material on exfoliated films caused by ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 297.905pt, "uncontrollable cleavage depth, which hinders the universal realization of freestanding films. ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 318pt, "Fundamentally, this technique relies on stress concentration to induce directional cleavage along ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 338.154pt, "weak material interfaces, thereby achieving film separation. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.02pt, 365.999pt, "2.1.3 Remote Epitaxy van der Waals Lift-Off")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.02pt, 393.902pt, "Remote epitaxy enables controllable thin-film growth and subsequent detachment by introducing ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 414.056pt, "a 2D material interlayer, such as transferred graphene, onto a single-crystal substrate. The process ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 434.164pt, "begins with transferring the 2D layer onto a pre-treated substrate (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(382.321pt, 434.164pt, "Fig. 4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(409.92pt, 434.164pt, "a), followed by epitaxial ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 454.318pt, "growth of the target film on top of it (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(240.355pt, 454.318pt, "Fig. 4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(266.94pt, 454.318pt, "c). Its functionality relies on two fundamental principles. ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 474.425pt, "In remote epitaxy, the lattice information of the substrate penetrates through the 2D material, ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 494.579pt, "directing the film to grow along the substrate orientation while the 2D layer suppresses strong ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 514.675pt, "chemical bonding, thereby preserving a van der Waals interface. In van der Waals lift-off, the weak ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 534.841pt, "interaction between the 2D layer and the substrate or film is exploited, enabling stress-free ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 554.936pt, "separation of the “2D layer/film” heterostructure without inducing damage (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(424.134pt, 554.936pt, "Fig. 4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(451.197pt, 554.936pt, "b). This process ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 575.102pt, "preserves film single crystallinity, and the resulting freestanding films can be directly utilized or ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 595.198pt, "transferred to arbitrary substrates for high-quality heterogeneous integration [")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(431.755pt, 595.198pt, "111")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(449.213pt, 595.198pt, ",")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(452.154pt, 595.198pt, "123-126")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(491.039pt, 595.198pt, "].  ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 623.101pt, "This technique’s feasibility originates from the substrate polarity penetration mechanism [")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#00b0f0"))
  #line-at(500.818pt, 623.101pt, "127")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.67pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(518.335pt, 623.101pt, "]. ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 643.255pt, "Studies indicate that material ionicity directly governs electrostatic potential penetration capability: ")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 663.362pt, "Low-ionicity materials (e.g., GaAs, ≈32%) penetrate only monolayer graphene, whereas high-")
  #line-at(70.02pt, 683.516pt, "ionicity materials (e.g., GaN, LiF) penetrate up to three layers, while covalent materials (e.g., Si, ")
